    "lawCode": "Amd §2504, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7385\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to exempting certain\npublic construction projects from certain restrictions\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo expand the City of New York's ability to use owner-controlled and\ncontractor-controlled or \"wrap-up\" insurance.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this act exempts the City of New York, the City School\nDistrict of the City of New York (often referred to as \"DOE\"), the New\nYork City Industrial Development Agency (\"NYCIDA\"), the New York City\nHealth and Hospitals Corporation (\"H+H\"), and the New York City Housing\nAuthority (\"NYCHA\") from the prohibition on using owner-controlled\ninsurance policies in connection with any contract the principal purpose\nof which is construction, as well as exempting those entities from using\ncontractor-controlled insurance policies that have an estimated cost of\nmore than $5 million. It also provides that any contract undertaken\npursuant to this authorization that costs $5 million or more is only\nauthorized if undertaken pursuant to a project labor agreement.\n\nSection two of the act includes reporting language requiring the author-\nized entities to report annually on the program, to provide updates on\nMWBE and other small business participation rates and providing qualita-\ntive analysis of the program's administration with respect to details\nlike safety incidents and dispute resolution.\n\nSection three of this act provides that this act would take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSection 2504 of the Insurance Law generally prohibits the state and\npublic corporations and authorities from requiring bidders for a public\nbuilding or construction contract to make an application to a particular\ninsurance company, agent, or broker for a surety bond or contract of\ninsurance. This paragraph also prohibits such an officer, employee, or\nperson acting on their behalf from requiring such bidders to purchase\nfrom a particular insurance company, agent, or broker such a surety bond\nor contract of insurance. It also prohibits such an officer, employee,\nor person acting on their behalf from negotiating, applying for, or\npurchasing such a surety bond or contract of insurance, with limited\nexceptions, where such surety bond or insurance could be obtained by a\nbidder, contractor, or subcontractor. This has the effect of preventing\ngeneral contractors and subcontractors from collaborating to have one\numbrella insurance policy for construction projects.\n\nHowever, small contractors, including many small businesses and MWBEs,\noften struggle to find competitive insurance options that meet the stan-\ndards required for public construction projects, and these contractors\nparticularly struggle to obtain insurance options that are affordable.\nHigh insurance premiums make these businesses less competitive as prime\ncontractors and subcontractors because they pass along increased costs\nto public owners in the form of higher bid prices. This legislation\nwould authorize, subject to certain conditions, New York City, DOE,\nNYCIDA, H+H, and economic development entities working on their behalf\nto use OCIPs and CCIPs, which are comprehensive wrap-up insurance\nprograms common in both private and public construction projects. This\nlegislation would also clarify NYCHA's ability to use OCIPs and CCIPs.\n\nUnder an OCIP, a single policy held by the owner covers the owner and\ncontractors of all levels for the entire project or group of projects.\nUnder a CCIP, the insurance policy is held by the construction manager\nor general contractor and covers the owner as well.\n\nIn a conventional project structure, in which many contractors provide\ninsurance to the public owner, disputes between different carriers over\nliability and coverage consume considerable time and resources, escalat-\ning costs. In contrast, an OCIP or CCIP can provide more efficient\ncoverage through a single policy. Further, when a public owner imple-\nments an OCIP or CCIP, the owner is relieved of the burden of monitoring\nthat each contractor maintains coverage if the project continues beyond\nthe length of an applicable policy.\n\nThe City argues that OCIPs and CCIPs also create safe worksites. In\ncontrast to a conventional project structure wherein contractors and\nsubcontractors hold multiple policies with separate safety requirements,\nOCIPs and CCIPs typically harmonize safety standards across contractors.\nAdditionally, the City argues that OCIPs and CCIPs allow for an inte-\ngrated data platform to facilitate safety data collection and review.\n\nCity contractors voluntarily chose to implement CCIPs on three separate\nprojects: the Police Academy in Queens, Public Safety Answering Center\nII in the Bronx, and Build-it-Back, the renovation of residential homes\nafter Hurricane Sandy. All three projects resulted in fewer incidents\nand claims than average, no deaths, and no serious injuries. All three\nprojects progressed over multiple years with continuous coverage. Addi-\ntionally, OCIPs and CCIPs are already successfully implemented by vari-\nous public entities in the State, including the New York City School\nConstruction Authority, the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey,\nand the Metropolitan Transportation Authority. During the previous\nlegislative session, Chapter 225 of the laws of 2022 authorized the New\nYork City Housing Preservation Trust to implement OCIPs and CCIPs.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNo negative impacts to the state or local governments anticipated.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
